UNIT bytes
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR bytes@3.1.2 index.js — the default export (format for a number, parse for text); reproduce the package exactly,
  accidents included (R5, R6, R11, R13 and the OUT-OF-DOMAIN surface)
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG bytes(value: json, ...options: json) -> text | f64 | none
ENVELOPE value is any json value: text (any Unicode text), f64 (NaN, +Infinity, -Infinity and -0 included), none, bool,
  list, map. The trailing slot holds zero or more json values; only the first is read (R2) and it is the OPTIONS value.
  The verified OPTIONS is either not a map (R2) or a map whose recognized keys, when present, hold: unit text | none;
  decimalPlaces f64 | none; fixedDecimals bool | none; thousandsSeparator text | none; unitSeparator text | none — with
  unit text that is not, ignoring letter case, "constructor" or "__proto__", and thousandsSeparator text containing no
  "$". Unrecognized keys are IN and ignored. Every other option value is OUT-OF-DOMAIN (its package behavior is stated
  there). Two inputs THROW and are IN: decimalPlaces outside 0..100 (R10) and a FILLED non-text unit (R8). A text
  numeral beyond the finite f64 range yields an infinite result and is IN (R4, R5).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Two-way byte-size conversion. Text is parsed to a count of bytes: a strict numeral-plus-unit form ("1.5kb")
  scales by a binary (1024-based) unit and floors; any other text is read leniently as a leading integer ("5b", "1024",
  "  12abc"). A number is formatted as text with a binary unit chosen by magnitude ("1.5KB"), shaped by the options
  unit, decimalPlaces, fixedDecimals, thousandsSeparator and unitSeparator. Anything else yields none.
BEHAVIOR
R1 The kind of value selects the direction: text -> PARSE (R4-R6, result f64 | none); f64 -> FORMAT (R7-R16, result text, (restated)
  none, or a throw); none, bool, list or map -> none, and no option is read.
R2 The first trailing argument, when there is one, is OPTIONS; any further trailing argument is ignored. OPTIONS is read
  only by FORMAT — PARSE never consults it. When OPTIONS is absent or is not a map, every option is ABSENT. Keys other
  than the five of TABLE OPTION are ignored.
R3 Vocabulary for an option value: ABSENT = the key is not present in the map; BLANK = none, false, the number zero (-0
  included), NaN, or empty text; FILLED = any other value (true, any other number, any non-empty text, any list or map,
  even an empty one). TABLE OPTION reads every option with these words.
R4 PARSE, STRICT form: when the WHOLE text matches STRICT (PINNED below) — an optional sign, one or more digits, an
  optional point with one or more digits, zero or more ordinary spaces, a unit word in ASCII letter case — the result is
  floor(scale × numeral): the numeral (sign, integer digits, fraction) read as the nearest f64, multiplied in f64 by
  UNIT_SCALE at the lowercased unit word, then the largest integer not above the product (toward negative infinity).
  "5.9kb" -> 6041, "-5.9kb" -> -6042, "+2gb" -> 2147483648, "5  kb" -> 5120. The unit word "b" is NOT in STRICT. Case
  is folded over the ASCII letters only ("1Mb", "1mB", "1 pB" match; a KELVIN SIGN is not a "k" here, unlike R8).
R5 PARSE, LENIENT form: any other text. Skip a leading run of LEADING_SPACE characters (PINNED below); then an optional
  single "+" or "-"; then the longest run of one or more ASCII decimal digits, which must start immediately (no space
  after the sign, no point before the first digit); no digit there -> none. Everything after the digit run is ignored,
  a fraction included. The result is the integer the run denotes, as the nearest f64 (beyond the finite range: an
  infinity), with the sign applied. "1.5b" -> 1, "-1.5" -> -1, "  1 kb  " -> 1, "5\tkb" -> 5, "0x10" -> 0, "1e3" -> 1,
  "1kb " -> 1, ".5kb" -> none, "- 5" -> none, "abc" -> none, "" -> none, "010" -> 10.
  CITED FIDELITY package — the fallback is the host's integer reading, so "b" and "B" suffixes, and every strict form with stray whitespace, truncate
R6 A "-" sign with a zero magnitude yields negative zero in both forms: "-0", "-00", "-0.5", "-0kb", "-0.0kb" -> -0;
  "+0", "0", "0kb" -> 0.
  CITED FIDELITY package — the sign survives the multiplication and the floor
R7 FORMAT: value NaN, +Infinity or -Infinity -> none, before any option is read (a bad option never throws for these).
R8 The unit. When OPTIONS.unit is text whose lowercase (Unicode default case mapping) is a key of UNIT_SCALE, the suffix
  is that text AS GIVEN (letter case kept: "Kb" -> "1Kb", "kb" -> "1kb", "b" -> "1024b") and the scale is the key's.
  When OPTIONS.unit is ABSENT, BLANK, or any other text ("xx", "BYTES", "KBB"), the suffix and scale come from TABLE
  AUTO_UNIT on |value|. When OPTIONS.unit is FILLED and not text (true, a nonzero number, a list, a map) -> throws.
  CITED FIDELITY package — the package applies a text operation to whatever it was given
R9 q = value ÷ scale, in f64 (so -0 stays -0 and a tiny magnitude may become zero).
R10 Decimal places d: ABSENT -> 2; none -> 0; false -> 0, true -> 1; a number -> truncated toward zero (NaN -> 0). After
  that, d below 0 or above 100 (an infinity included) -> throws. 1.7 -> 1; -0.5 -> 0; 100.5 -> 100; -1 and 101 throw.
R11 FIXED rendering of |q| with exactly d digits after the point (no point when d is 0): the d-place decimal nearest to
  the EXACT binary value of q — not to a shorter decimal reading of it — and when two are equally near, the larger
  one. The integer part is written in full, "0" when it is zero, with no grouping yet. A "-" is prefixed exactly when
  q < 0: a negative q that rounds to zero still shows it ("-0", "-0.00"), a negative zero q does not ("0B" for -0).
  CITED FIDELITY package — 1.005 renders "1" (its binary value lies below the tie); 0.125 renders "0.13"; 1.125 renders "1.13"; 2.5 with d 0 renders "3"; -0.5 with d 0 renders "-1"; 100.5 with d 0 renders "101"
R12 When |q| is at least 10^21 the rendering is instead the shortest round-trip form: the fewest significant digits
  that read back as exactly |q|, written as the first digit, then — only when more digits follow — a point and the
  rest, then "e+" and the decimal exponent without padding (10^21 -> "1e+21"); d has no effect on the digits, though
  R10's throw still applies; "-" is prefixed when q < 0.
  CITED FIDELITY package — 1e21 with unit "B" -> "1e+21B"; 1e40 -> "8.881784197001253e+24PB"; -1e40 -> "-8.881784197001253e+24PB"
R13 TRIM, unless fixedDecimals is on (TABLE OPTION): TABLE TRIM is applied to the rendering, keyed on the characters
  after its point. It sees characters, not digits: in the R12 form a trailing zero of the EXPONENT is trimmed too
  ("1.5e+210" -> "1.5e+21", "1.5e+300" -> "1.5e+3", "1.05e+300" stays). "1.50" -> "1.5", "1.00" -> "1", "1.05" stays,
  "1.050" stays, "1.0500" stays, "1.2030" stays.
  CITED FIDELITY package — the trimmer only recognizes a zero-free run before the zeros
R14 GROUPING, when thousandsSeparator is text (TABLE OPTION): in the LEADING PART of the rendering — everything before
  its first point, or all of it when it has no point — the separator is inserted between two adjacent digits of a
  digit run wherever the count of digits that follow the position within that run is a positive multiple of three.
  Nothing else moves: the "-", the point and everything after it are untouched, and the R12 form is never grouped
  (its runs are at most three digits). "-1234567" -> "-1,234,567", "1000" -> "1,000", "100" -> "100",
  "1205632705.2" with "." -> "1.205.632.705.2".
R15 The result is the rendering, then the unitSeparator text (empty when ABSENT or BLANK), then the suffix of R8.
R16 When both R8 and R10 would throw, R8's throw is the one raised (the unit is read before the decimal places).
TABLE AUTO_UNIT — |value| -> suffix, scale key of UNIT_SCALE; first row whose condition holds (R8)
  |value| >= 1125899906842624 (2^50) -> "PB", pb
  |value| >= 1099511627776 (2^40)    -> "TB", tb
  |value| >= 1073741824 (2^30)       -> "GB", gb
  |value| >= 1048576 (2^20)          -> "MB", mb
  |value| >= 1024 (2^10)             -> "KB", kb
  otherwise                          -> "B", b
TABLE OPTION — key -> reading of its value in R3 words (ABSENT / BLANK / FILLED)
  unit               | ABSENT or BLANK -> AUTO_UNIT | text that lowercases to a UNIT_SCALE key -> that text, verbatim, with that scale | other text -> AUTO_UNIT | FILLED non-text -> throws (R8)
  decimalPlaces      | ABSENT -> 2 | none -> 0 | false -> 0, true -> 1 | f64 -> truncated toward zero, NaN -> 0, then outside 0..100 -> throws (R10) | text, list, map -> OUT-OF-DOMAIN
  fixedDecimals      | ABSENT or BLANK -> off | FILLED -> on (R13)
  thousandsSeparator | ABSENT or BLANK -> no grouping | text -> that text (R14) | FILLED non-text -> OUT-OF-DOMAIN
  unitSeparator      | ABSENT or BLANK -> empty | text -> that text (R15) | FILLED non-text -> OUT-OF-DOMAIN
TABLE TRIM — F = the characters after the rendering's point (R13); first row that applies
  the rendering has no point                                              -> unchanged
  every character of F is "0"                                             -> the point and F are removed
  F is one or more characters none of which is "0", then one or more "0"  -> those trailing zeros are removed
  otherwise (a "0" occurs somewhere before a later non-"0" character)     -> unchanged
PINNED grammar STRICT = [ "+" | "-" ] DIGIT+ [ "." DIGIT+ ] SPACE* UNIT CITED R4 — bytes@3.1.2 parseRegExp; DIGIT = the ten ASCII digits, SPACE = the ordinary space character only (no tab, no other whitespace), UNIT = one of "kb" "mb" "gb" "tb" "pb" with each letter in ASCII upper or lower case (no other case folding); the grammar must cover the whole text, with nothing before or after
PINNED charset LEADING_SPACE = { the ASCII controls TAB, LF, VT, FF and CR, the space, no-break space, zero width no-break space, line separator, paragraph separator, and every character of general category Space Separator (Zs) } CITED R5 — the whitespace the package's host skips before a numeral; next line (NEL) and the Mongolian vowel separator are NOT in it
PINNED map UNIT_SCALE = { b: 1, kb: 1024, mb: 1048576, gb: 1073741824, tb: 1099511627776, pb: 1125899906842624 } CITED FIDELITY package — bytes@3.1.2 map: binary (1024-based) scales keyed by the lowercase unit word
INVARIANT for text value the result is none, an integer-valued f64 (negative zero included), or an infinity; OPTIONS never
  changes it.
INVARIANT for f64 value the result is none exactly when value is not finite; otherwise it is text ending in the suffix
  of R8, or a throw from R8 or R10.
OUT-OF-DOMAIN OPTIONS.unit text equal, ignoring letter case, to "constructor" or "__proto__": the package finds an
  inherited member under that key and divides by it — the rendering is the text "NaN" followed by the unitSeparator
  and the unit as given ("NaNconstructor", "NaN constructor").
  A FILLED non-text thousandsSeparator or unitSeparator is rendered as text by the host and then used as R14/R15 say:
  true -> "true"; a number -> its shortest round-trip decimal rendering (integers without a point: 5 -> "5", -20 ->
  "-20"; 1.35 -> "1.35"; beyond 10^21 or below 10^-7 the host's exponent form, "1e+21", "1e-7"); a list -> its items
  rendered the same way joined by ",", a none item rendering as empty text (so an empty list, or a list holding only
  none, behaves like no separator); a map -> "[object Object]".
  A thousandsSeparator text containing "$" followed by "$", "&", "`", "'" or "1" is handed by the package to its host's
  pattern replacement: "$$" inserts "$", "$&" inserts nothing, "$`" inserts the digits before the insertion point, "$'"
  the digits after it, "$1" the last three digits of the run; any other "$" is literal ("$" -> "1$536$000", "$2" ->
  "1$2536$2000").
  A decimalPlaces text is read by the host as a numeral (surrounding whitespace allowed, exponent and "0x" forms
  allowed, empty or blank -> 0, unreadable -> 0) and then treated as a number by R10 ("1" -> 1, "abc" -> 0); a list
  with exactly one item reads as that item would, any other list -> 0; a map -> 0.
  CITED FIDELITY package — every statement of this stanza was probed against bytes@3.1.2
ORACLE value -> oracles/bytes.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 fb575fae9fa5c8ae05d32429529c78ab8a3a8c1b100a9f76f2fe038619aff64d
NOTE The SIG carries OPTIONS in a trailing slot because the abstract call is 1-ary or 2-ary and the canonical vector form
  has no optional parameter; natively the package's second parameter is optional and each language file maps it. A call
  with three or more arguments is in scope (R2).
NOTE 16 frozen + 43 held-out, captured once from the real package, never regenerated. Every vector is a json value, so
  all 59 are language-neutral; the four holding NaN, +Infinity, -Infinity or -0 as value are narrowed by any language
  whose transport cannot carry them.
SCOPE IN: R1-R16, the three tables, the three pins and the OUT-OF-DOMAIN surface, over json values and the trailing-
  argument call shape. OUT: the named exports format and parse as separately callable units (parse's pass-through of a
  number argument is unreachable through the default export and is not stated); host-only values (the js file's
  widening); nothing else of bytes@3.1.2 is excluded.
PROVENANCE bytes@3.1.2 (visionmedia/bytes.js, MIT), pkgs/node_modules/bytes/index.js: bytes (module.exports), format,
  parse, map, parseRegExp, formatDecimalsRegExp, formatThousandsRegExp. Cold re-cut 2026-09-23 into the v0.5 contract
  register from the real source and the pre-captured oracle only; every rule, table row and OUT-OF-DOMAIN statement was
  probed against the real package before it was written.
